<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · river</p><h1 class="enc-title">Saar (river)</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Saar is a river of north-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> and western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, about 246 kilometres long, which rises in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vosges">Vosges</a> and flows north past <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saarbrucken">Saarbrücken</a> to join the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moselle">Moselle</a> near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trier">Trier</a>. Its valley and the surrounding <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coal">coal</a> basin gave their name to the Saar region, separated from Germany under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/league-of-nations">League of Nations</a> from 1920 to 1935 and under French control after 1945. Today it is the core of the German state of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saarland">Saarland</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/saar-river.jpg" alt="Saar (river)" width="320" height="156" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Niesefrosch · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Saarschleife.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
